
  <section xml:id="Math2301Day14" xml:lang="en-US">
    <title>We're Doing This Eigen?</title>

    <!-- ================================================================ -->
    <subsection xml:id="sec-day14-review">
      <title>Review</title>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-preimage-subspace">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>T: U \to V</m> be a linear transformation between vector spaces.
            Let <m>W \subseteq V</m> be a subspace.
            Let <m>T^{-1}(W) = \{\vec{u} \in U: T(\vec{u}) \in W\}</m>.
            Show that <m>T^{-1}(W)</m> is a subspace of <m>U</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ================================================================ -->
    <subsection xml:id="sec-eigenstuff">
      <title>Eigenstuff</title>

      <p>
        With all of our new tools from orthogonality, we are now going to revisit
        eigenvectors from the end of math 2000.
        The rest of the course is focused on building and then extending this theory
        from diagonalizable square matrices to encompass every matrix.
        We will conclude with a core application to data science.
      </p>

      <p>
        First let's remind ourselves about eigenvectors.
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-eigenvector">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>V</m> be a vector space and <m>T: V \to V</m> be an operator.
            A nonzero vector <m>\vec{v}</m> is an <term>eigenvector</term> of <m>T</m>
            with <term>eigenvalue</term> <m>\lambda\in\mathbb{R}</m> if
            <me>
              T(\vec{v})=\lambda \vec{v}.
            </me>
            The <term>eigenspace</term> for <m>\lambda</m> is the set of eigenvectors
            with eigenvalue <m>\lambda</m>, together with the zero vector.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-eigenspace-kernel">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>V</m> be a vector space and <m>T</m> an operator with eigenvalue
            <m>\lambda</m> and <m>I:V \to V</m> be the identity operator.
            Then the eigenspace of <m>\lambda</m> is precisely
            <me>\ker(T-\lambda I).</me>
            In particular, it is a linear subspace of <m>V</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            A vector <m>\vec{v} \in \ker(T - \lambda I)</m> if and only if
            <me>(T - \lambda I)\vec{v} = T\vec{v} - \lambda \vec{v} = \vec{0},</me>
            which is true if and only if <m>T \vec{v} = \lambda \vec{v}</m>.
            Thus, the eigenspace is indeed a linear subspace.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <p>
        For any set <m>S</m>, a fixed point of a function <m>f: S \to S</m> is a point
        <m>x \in S</m> such that <m>f(x) = x</m>.
        For example, for any operator, <m>\vec{0}</m> is a fixed point.
        Eigenspaces are generalizations of fixed points.
        Instead of just fixing a point, you fix a subspace.
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-invariant-subspace">
        <statement>
          <p>
            A subspace <m>U\subseteq V</m> is <term>invariant under <m>T</m></term>
            if <m>T(U)\subseteq U</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        Eigenspaces are a particular kind of invariant subspace.
        Recalling from math 2000, for a matrix <m>A</m>, an eigenvector is a solution
        to the equation <m>A\vec{v}=\lambda \vec{v}</m> or equivalently
        <me>
          (A-\lambda I)\vec{v}=\vec{0}.
        </me>
        In that class, we then came to the conclusion that a nonzero solution exists
        exactly when <m>A-\lambda I</m> is singular.
        This happens precisely when
        <me>
          \det(A-\lambda I)=0.
        </me>
        This function <m>\det(A- \lambda I)</m> is a polynomial called the
        <term>characteristic polynomial</term> of the matrix, and the eigenvalues
        are the roots of that polynomial.
        Then computationally finding eigenvalues came down to finding roots of
        characteristic polynomials, and you'd find eigenvectors after by solving the
        linear system <m>(A-\lambda I )\vec{v} = \vec{0}</m> for each eigenvalue
        <m>\lambda</m>.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ================================================================ -->
    <subsection xml:id="sec-eigenvectors-bases">
      <title>Eigenvectors and Bases</title>

      <p>
        From the definition, any two eigenspaces for distinct eigenvalues only
        intersect at the zero vector.
        In fact, we get a stronger property.
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-distinct-eigenvalues-independent">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>V</m> be a vector space and <m>T</m> be a linear operator.
            Nonzero eigenvectors corresponding to distinct eigenvalues are linearly
            independent.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            We do this by induction.
            Certainly, one eigenvector is independent.
            Suppose that any set of <m>n</m> eigenvectors with distinct eigenvalues is
            linearly independent.
            We want to show it is true for <m>n+1</m> eigenvectors.
          </p>

          <p>
            Let <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{n+1}</m> be eigenvectors each with
            distinct eigenvalues.
            Suppose for the sake of contradiction that they are linearly dependent.
            By our inductive hypothesis <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{n}</m> is
            linearly independent.
            Since <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{n+1}</m> is linearly dependent, we
            must then have that <m>\text{span}(\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{n+1})</m>
            is <m>n</m>-dimensional.
          </p>

          <p>
            Define the linear transformation <m>A: \mathbb{R}^{n+1} \to V</m> by
            <m>A(\vec{e}_{i}) = \vec{v}_{i}</m> and extending linearly.
            Then the image is precisely
            <m>\text{span}(\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{n+1})</m>.
            Thus, the kernel is one dimensional by the rank-nullity theorem.
          </p>

          <p>
            Hence, there is a unique nonzero vector up to rescaling
            <m>\vec{a} \in \mathbb{R}^{n+1}</m> such that
            <me>\sum_{i=1}^{n+1} a_{i} \vec{v}_{i} = A(\vec{a}) = \vec{0}.</me>
            In particular, since each <m>\vec{v}_{i}</m> is nonzero, there exist
            <m>j \neq k</m> such that <m>a_{j}, a_{k} \neq 0</m>.
          </p>

          <p>
            Finally now we use that each <m>\vec{v}_{i}</m> is an eigenvector with
            eigenvalue <m>\lambda_{i}</m> to see that
            <me>
              A(a_{1}\lambda_{1}, \dots, a_{n+1}\lambda_{n+1})
              = \sum_{i=1}^{n+1} a_{i}\lambda_{i} \vec{v}_{i}
              = \sum_{i=1}^{n+1} a_{i}T(\vec{v}_{i})
              = T\left(\sum_{i=1}^{n+1} a_{i} \vec{v}_{i} \right)
              = T(\vec{0}) = \vec{0}.
            </me>
            Thus, by the uniqueness of <m>\vec{a}</m>, we must have that there exists
            <m>\lambda \in \mathbb{R}</m> such that
            <me>a_{i} \lambda_{i} = a_{i} \lambda</me>
            for all <m>1 \leq i \leq n+1</m>.
            In particular, <m>\lambda_{j}a_{j} = \lambda a_{j}</m> and
            <m>\lambda_{k}a_{k} = \lambda a_{k}</m>.
            Since <m>a_{j}</m> and <m>a_{k}</m> are both nonzero, this implies
            <m>\lambda_{j} = \lambda_{k}</m>, a contradiction.
            The only assumption we made was that
            <m>\vec{v}_{1}, \dots, \vec{v}_{n+1}</m> were dependent.
            Hence, the contradiction implies they are independent, and induction
            completes the proof.
          </p>
          <!--
            Alternative proof, commented out in the original LaTeX source:

            For the base case, one nonzero eigenvector is linearly independent as any
            set containing only one nonzero vector is.

            Let have distinct eigenvalues $\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_k$ and
            \[ a_1v_1+\cdots+a_kv_k=0. \]
            Apply $T-\lambda_kI$ to obtain
            \[ a_1(\lambda_1-\lambda_k)v_1+\cdots+a_{k-1}(\lambda_{k-1}-\lambda_k)v_{k-1}=0. \]
            By induction, every $a_i(\lambda_i-\lambda_k)=0$ for $i<k$. Distinctness
            makes each factor $\lambda_i-\lambda_k$ nonzero, so $a_1=\cdots=a_{k-1}=0$.
            The original relation then gives $a_k=0$.
          -->
        </proof>
      </theorem>

      <!-- Placeholder from the original source: \figEigenDirections (macro was never defined). -->
    </subsection>

    <!-- ================================================================ -->
    <subsection xml:id="sec-diagonalization">
      <title>Diagonalization</title>

      <p>
        One of the primary uses of eigenvalues and eigenvectors is that they allow us
        to treat almost any matrix as a diagonal matrix via a process called
        diagonalization.
      </p>

      <definition xml:id="def-diagonalizable">
        <statement>
          <p>
            An operator <m>T: V \to V</m> is <term>diagonalizable</term> over
            <m>\mathbb{R}</m> if <m>V</m> has a basis consisting of real eigenvectors
            of <m>T</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </definition>

      <p>
        We call a basis of eigenvectors an <term>eigenbasis</term>.
        Let <m>\mathcal B=(\vec{v}_1,\ldots,\vec{v}_n)</m> be an eigenbasis for an
        operator <m>T:V \to V</m> with <m>T(\vec{v}_i)=\lambda_i\vec{v}_i</m>.
        Then recall the notation for how to go from an operator to a matrix in a
        basis.
        We just write the coefficients of the image of each basis element in that
        basis.
        Well, <m>T(\vec{v}_{i}) = \lambda_{i} \vec{v}_{i}</m>, meaning that the
        <m>i</m>th column is just <m>\lambda_{i}</m> in the <m>i</m>th entry and
        <m>0</m>'s otherwise.
        Thus, written in an eigenbasis, the operator is a diagonal matrix:
        <me>
          [T]_{\mathcal B}=\text{diag}(\lambda_1,\ldots,\lambda_n).
        </me>
        You may recall that for matrices, using the change of basis formula, if
        <m>P</m> has columns given by an eigenbasis for a matrix <m>A</m>, then
        <me>
          A=PDP^{-1},
        </me>
        where <m>D</m> is the diagonal matrix of corresponding eigenvalues.
        You may recall from math 2000 that not every matrix is diagonalizable.
        Here is a characterization in terms of eigenspaces that follows from what
        we've shown so far:
      </p>

      <theorem xml:id="thm-diagonalizable-dimensions">
        <statement>
          <p>
            An <m>n\times n</m> real matrix is diagonalizable if and only if the sum of
            the dimensions of its real eigenspaces is <m>n</m>.
          </p>
        </statement>
        <proof>
          <p>
            Eigenspaces for distinct eigenvalues combine independently by the
            preceding theorem.
            Their union of bases forms an eigenbasis exactly when the total number of
            basis vectors is <m>n</m>.
          </p>
        </proof>
      </theorem>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ================================================================ -->
    <subsection xml:id="sec-iterated-powers">
      <title>Iterated Powers</title>

      <p>
        If <m>A=PDP^{-1}</m>, then
        <me>
          A^k=PD^kP^{-1},\qquad
          D^k=\text{diag}(\lambda_1^k,\ldots,\lambda_n^k).
        </me>
        For an initial state <m>\vec{x}_0=\sum_i c_i\vec{v}_i</m>,
        <me>
          A^k\vec{x}_0=\sum_i c_i\lambda_i^k\vec{v}_i.
        </me>
        Each eigen-direction evolves independently.
      </p>
    </subsection>

    <!-- ================================================================ -->
    <subsection xml:id="sec-day14-exercises">
      <title>Exercises</title>
      <exercise xml:id="ex-invariant-lines">
        <statement>
            <p>
              Consider the rotation
              <m>R = \begin{pmatrix} 0 &amp; -1 \\ 1 &amp; 0 \end{pmatrix}</m>.
              Show that <m>R</m> has no invariant lines and no real eigenvalues. Interpret this geometrically.
            </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>


      <exercise xml:id="ex-eigen-inner-product">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>T: V \to V</m> be an operator on an inner product space.
            Let <m>\vec{v}</m> be an eigenvector of <m>T</m> with eigenvalue
            <m>\lambda</m>.
            Show that
            <me>\langle \vec{v}, T(\vec{v})\rangle = \langle T(\vec{v}), \vec{v}\rangle = \lambda \|\vec{v}\|^{2}.</me>
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-transpose-adjoint">
        <statement>
          <p>
            Let <m>A</m> be any <m>n \times n</m> matrix.
            Show that <m>\langle \vec{u}, A\vec{v}\rangle = \langle A^{\intercal} \vec{u}, \vec{v}\rangle</m>
            for all <m>\vec{u}, \vec{v} \in \mathbb{R}^n</m>, where the inner product
            is the dot product.
          </p>
        </statement>
      </exercise>

      <p>
        We call a matrix <m>A</m> <term>symmetric</term> if <m>A = A^{\intercal}</m>.
      </p>

      <exercise xml:id="ex-symmetric-orthogonal">
        <introduction>
          <p>
            Suppose that a matrix <m>A</m> is symmetric.
          </p>
        </introduction>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Show that <m>\langle \vec{u}, A\vec{v}\rangle = \langle A \vec{u},\vec{v}\rangle</m>,
              where the inner product here is the regular old dot product.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
        <task>
          <statement>
            <p>
              Show that eigenvectors of a symmetric matrix with distinct eigenvalues
              are orthogonal.
            </p>
          </statement>
        </task>
      </exercise>


    </subsection>

    

  
  </section>
